
How to enable the SpamAssassin LDAP self-test
---------------------------------------------

t/spamd_ldap.t runs only when the file t/do_ldap exists. It needs the Perl
module Net::LDAP and an LDAP server on localhost holding the o=stooges test
data in ldap/sa_test.ldif, with cn=StoogeAdmin,o=stooges as its admin and
secret1 as the password (ONLY FOR TESTING! DO NOT DO THIS ON A LIVE SERVER!).

These steps are for an OpenLDAP server configured through cn=config, as
current packages are. The commands are for Debian and Ubuntu; other systems
differ in package names, in the user slapd runs as and in where it keeps its
databases (edit olcDbDirectory in ldap/sa_test_db.ldif to match).


- Install the server, the client tools and Net::LDAP:

    sudo apt-get install slapd ldap-utils libnet-ldap-perl


- Add the 'spamassassin' attribute that holds the user preferences:

    sudo ldapadd -Q -Y EXTERNAL -H ldapi:/// -f ldap/sa_test_schema.ldif


- Create the o=stooges database:

    sudo install -d -o openldap -g openldap -m 0700 /var/lib/ldap/stooges
    sudo ldapadd -Q -Y EXTERNAL -H ldapi:/// -f ldap/sa_test_db.ldif


- Load the test data:

    ldapadd -x -H ldap://127.0.0.1 -D "cn=StoogeAdmin,o=stooges" -w secret1 \
      -f ldap/sa_test.ldif


- Create the test flag file:

    touch t/do_ldap


- Now "make test" will test against the LDAP server as well. You can also
  run just that test with: make test TEST_FILES="t/spamd_ldap.t"
